select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-082","paper":1,"topic":"Project management","kind":"problem","front":"PERT time estimates in weeks (optimistic a, most likely m, pessimistic b): A (start) 2-4-6; B (after A) 3-5-13; C (start) 4-6-8; D (after C) 2-3-4; E (after B and D) 1-2-3. Find the expected time of each activity, the critical path, the expected project duration, and its standard deviation.","back":"te: A 4, B 6, C 6, D 3, E 2. Critical path A–B–E = 12 weeks; σ ≈ 1.83 weeks.","explain":"PERT uses a beta-distribution approximation: te = (a + 4m + b)/6 and variance = ((b − a)/6)². Add up the variances along the critical path and take the square root to get the project's standard deviation, which is used to estimate the probability of meeting a deadline.","steps":[{"prompt":"Expected times.","answer":"A (2+16+6)/6 = 4; B (3+20+13)/6 = 6; C (4+24+8)/6 = 6; D (2+12+4)/6 = 3; E (1+8+3)/6 = 2","explain":"te = (a + 4m + b) ÷ 6."},{"prompt":"Path lengths.","answer":"A–B–E = 4+6+2 = 12; C–D–E = 6+3+2 = 11","explain":"The critical path is the longest path."},{"prompt":"Variances on the critical path.","answer":"A (4/6)² = 0.444; B (10/6)² = 2.778; E (2/6)² = 0.111; total = 3.333","explain":"σ² = ((b − a)/6)²."},{"prompt":"Project standard deviation.","answer":"σ = √3.333 = 1.83 weeks; expected duration = 12 weeks","explain":"Add variances (not standard deviations), then take the square root."}],"flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1081},
{"id":"p1-083","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"What principle is energy Monitoring & Targeting (M&T) based on?","back":"'You can't manage what you don't measure.' Energy use is related to its driver (production, degree-days), and actual consumption is compared with expected consumption so that deviations can be found and acted on.","explain":"The M&T elements are recording (measuring), analysing (correlating with production), comparing (against a baseline or target), setting targets, monitoring, reporting and controlling. M&T turns energy from an overhead into a managed variable cost.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q50","order":1082},
{"id":"p1-084","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"How is fixed (non-production) energy consumption found from data?","back":"Plot energy (y) against production (x) on an XY scatter chart and fit E = m·P + c. The intercept c is the fixed energy consumption, and the slope m is the variable energy per unit of production.","explain":"Fixed energy (lighting, HVAC, idle running, losses) is used regardless of output. That is why SEC rises at low capacity utilisation. Reducing c is often the cheapest saving.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q17","order":1083},
{"id":"p1-085","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"Why is an energy baseline established in M&T?","back":"To fix a reference point (expected energy for a given production) against which later energy performance improvements are measured.","explain":"The baseline is usually a regression E = mP + c from a representative period before the energy-saving measures. The baseline and energy performance indicators are core requirements of ISO 50001 and of M&V for ESCO contracts.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q19","order":1084},
{"id":"p1-086","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"What is CUSUM, and how do you interpret its graph?","back":"CUSUM = cumulative sum of (actual − predicted) energy, period by period. A falling line means savings (actual below predicted). A rising line means excess consumption. A horizontal line means actual = predicted (no change in performance).","explain":"Predicted energy comes from the baseline equation, e.g. E = 2.2·P + 10,000. The final CUSUM value gives the total savings (if negative) over the period. Changes in slope mark when performance changed, for example when a measure was implemented or a fault appeared.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q43, L4; BEE 2024 P1 L1","order":1085},
{"id":"p1-087","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"problem","front":"The baseline relationship for a plant is E = 120·P + 20,000 (E in kWh, P in tonnes). After an energy-saving programme, the monthly data (production in t / actual energy in kWh) are: Apr 400/66,000; May 420/70,000; Jun 380/63,000; Jul 450/73,500; Aug 410/67,000; Sep 390/64,500. Build the CUSUM table and find the total savings.","back":"CUSUM after six months = −10,000 kWh, i.e. 10,000 kWh saved.","explain":"For each month, predicted energy comes from the baseline equation. The monthly difference (actual − predicted) is added up cumulatively. A steadily falling CUSUM line confirms sustained savings; a flat stretch would mean performance had returned to baseline.","steps":[{"prompt":"Predicted energy each month (120P + 20,000).","answer":"Apr 68,000; May 70,400; Jun 65,600; Jul 74,000; Aug 69,200; Sep 66,800 kWh","explain":"Use the baseline regression."},{"prompt":"Difference (actual − predicted).","answer":"Apr −2,000; May −400; Jun −2,600; Jul −500; Aug −2,200; Sep −2,300","explain":"Negative = consumed less than expected."},{"prompt":"Cumulative sum.","answer":"−2,000; −2,400; −5,000; −5,500; −7,700; −10,000","explain":"CUSUMₙ = CUSUMₙ₋₁ + (Eₐ − Eₚ)."},{"prompt":"Interpret.","answer":"Total savings over 6 months = 10,000 kWh; the CUSUM line slopes down throughout.","explain":"A downward slope means savings; a horizontal line means no change."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 L4; BEE 2024 P1 L1 (modified)","order":1086},
{"id":"p1-088","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"fact","front":"Which six greenhouse gases are covered by the Kyoto Protocol?","back":"Carbon dioxide (CO₂), methane (CH₄), nitrous oxide (N₂O), hydrofluorocarbons (HFCs), perfluorocarbons (PFCs) and sulphur hexafluoride (SF₆).","explain":"CO₂ is the largest contributor by volume. The fluorinated gases have very high Global Warming Potentials. Emissions are reported in tonnes of CO₂-equivalent (tCO₂e) = mass × GWP.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1087},
{"id":"p1-089","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"fact","front":"Which greenhouse gas has the longest atmospheric lifetime and very high GWP?","back":"Sulphur hexafluoride (SF₆), with an atmospheric lifetime of about 3,200 years and a GWP in the tens of thousands relative to CO₂.","explain":"SF₆ is used as an insulating gas in high-voltage switchgear, so leak prevention and recovery matter. PFCs also last thousands of years. CO₂ has no single lifetime, because it is removed by several processes over very different timescales.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q7","order":1088},
{"id":"p1-090","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"fact","front":"What causes stratospheric ozone depletion, and what does the ozone layer filter?","back":"Mainly chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) and other halogenated compounds. The ozone layer absorbs harmful UV-B radiation.","explain":"Chlorine released from CFCs by UV light destroys ozone catalytically. The Montreal Protocol (1987) phased out CFCs and is phasing out HCFCs, and the Kigali Amendment covers HFCs. Ozone depletion and global warming are separate problems, but some gases contribute to both.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q8; BEE 2024 P1 Q29","order":1089},
{"id":"p1-091","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"fact","front":"What are the basic objectives of sustainable development?","back":"Economic security and prosperity, social development and advancement, and environmental sustainability, all three together.","explain":"The Brundtland definition: development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs. Energy efficiency supports all three objectives.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q26","order":1090},
{"id":"p1-092","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"fact","front":"What is carbon sequestration?","back":"Capturing CO₂ from point sources (e.g. power plants) or the atmosphere and storing it long term, for example in geological formations, oceans, or biomass and soils.","explain":"Carbon Capture, Utilisation and Storage (CCUS) handles point sources. Forests and soils act as natural carbon sinks.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q18","order":1091},
{"id":"p1-093","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"fact","front":"How do you convert fuel carbon content to CO₂ emissions?","back":"CO₂ (kg) = mass of carbon burnt (kg) × 44/12. Each kg of carbon gives 3.67 kg of CO₂.","explain":"The 44/12 comes from molecular weights: CO₂ = 44, C = 12. Example: 1,000 kg of natural gas with 74% carbon → 740 × 3.67 ≈ 2,713 kg CO₂. For electricity, multiply the kWh saved by the grid emission factor, e.g. 1,000 kWh × 0.8 kg/kWh = 800 kg CO₂.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 L6; BEE 2024 P1 Q1","order":1092},
{"id":"p1-094","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"fact","front":"What are India's updated NDC targets under the Paris Agreement (submitted 2022), and its net-zero year?","back":"Reduce the emissions intensity of GDP by 45% by 2030 from the 2005 level; reach about 50% of cumulative installed electric power capacity from non-fossil sources by 2030; net-zero emissions by 2070.","explain":"The Paris Agreement (COP21, 2015) aims to keep warming well below 2°C and to pursue 1.5°C, using nationally determined contributions. It followed the Kyoto Protocol (1997), which set binding targets only for developed countries and created the Clean Development Mechanism.","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":1093},
{"id":"p1-095","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"problem","front":"A 50 MW coal-fired power plant raises its efficiency from 30% to 33% through renovation and modernisation. Coal GCV is 3,600 kcal/kg, the plant runs 8,000 h/year at full load, and the emission factor is 1.5 kg CO₂ per kg of coal. Find the annual coal saving and the CO₂ avoided.","back":"Coal saving ≈ 28,956 t/yr; CO₂ avoided ≈ 43,434 t/yr.","explain":"Heat rate (kcal/kWh) = 860 ÷ efficiency, and specific coal consumption = heat rate ÷ GCV. The fall in specific coal consumption multiplied by generation gives the coal saved, and the emission factor converts that to CO₂ avoided. A 3-point efficiency gain cuts fuel use by about 9%.","steps":[{"prompt":"Specific coal consumption before.","answer":"Heat rate = 860/0.30 = 2,866.7 kcal/kWh; SCC = 2,866.7/3,600 = 0.7963 kg/kWh","explain":"SCC = 860 ÷ (η × GCV)."},{"prompt":"Specific coal consumption after.","answer":"860/0.33 = 2,606.1 kcal/kWh; SCC = 0.7239 kg/kWh","explain":""},{"prompt":"Annual generation.","answer":"50,000 kW × 8,000 h = 4×10⁸ kWh","explain":"Energy = capacity × hours."},{"prompt":"Coal saved.","answer":"(0.7963 − 0.7239) × 4×10⁸ = 2.896×10⁷ kg ≈ 28,956 t/yr","explain":"Saving = ΔSCC × generation."},{"prompt":"CO₂ avoided.","answer":"28,956 × 1.5 = 43,434 t CO₂/yr","explain":"CO₂ = coal saved × emission factor."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 S2 (modified)","order":1094},
{"id":"p1-096","paper":1,"topic":"PAT scheme & carbon market","kind":"fact","front":"What is the PAT scheme, and under which mission was it launched?","back":"Perform, Achieve and Trade: a market-based mechanism under the National Mission for Enhanced Energy Efficiency (NMEEE), one of the 8 missions of the National Action Plan on Climate Change (NAPCC, 2008). It sets mandatory SEC-reduction targets for Designated Consumers.","explain":"Plants that beat their target are issued Energy Saving Certificates (ESCerts). Plants that fall short must buy ESCerts or pay a penalty. ESCerts are traded on the power exchanges (IEX and PXIL).","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":1095},
{"id":"p1-097","paper":1,"topic":"PAT scheme & carbon market","kind":"fact","front":"What is one ESCert worth?","back":"1 Energy Saving Certificate (ESCert) = 1 tonne of oil equivalent (toe) of energy saved beyond the PAT target.","explain":"Number of ESCerts = (target SEC − achieved SEC) × production in the baseline year, expressed in toe. PAT Cycle I (2012–15) covered 478 DCs in 8 sectors with a target of about 6.686 Mtoe, and achieved about 8.67 Mtoe.","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":1096},
{"id":"p1-098","paper":1,"topic":"PAT scheme & carbon market","kind":"fact","front":"Name the four initiatives under the National Mission for Enhanced Energy Efficiency (NMEEE).","back":"1. PAT (Perform, Achieve and Trade). 2. MTEE (Market Transformation for Energy Efficiency). 3. EEFP (Energy Efficiency Financing Platform). 4. FEEED (Framework for Energy Efficient Economic Development).","explain":"PAT sets and trades efficiency targets. MTEE speeds up the shift to efficient appliances. EEFP helps finance institutions fund EE projects. FEEED provides fiscal instruments such as risk guarantee and venture funds.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1097},
{"id":"p1-099","paper":1,"topic":"PAT scheme & carbon market","kind":"fact","front":"What is the Carbon Credit Trading Scheme (CCTS) 2023 for the Indian Carbon Market?","back":"Notified in 2023 under the EC (Amendment) Act 2022. It has a compliance mechanism, where obligated entities get GHG-emission-intensity targets, and an offset mechanism for voluntary projects. 1 carbon credit certificate = 1 tCO₂e reduced or removed.","explain":"BEE is the administrator. Grid Controller of India acts as the registry, CERC regulates trading on power exchanges, and a National Steering Committee for the Indian Carbon Market (NSC-ICM) oversees it. Obligated sectors largely carry over from PAT, moving from energy (toe) targets to emission-intensity (tCO₂e per unit of product) targets.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1098},
{"id":"p2-001","paper":2,"topic":"Fuels","kind":"fact","front":"What is the difference between Gross Calorific Value (GCV) and Net Calorific Value (NCV)?","back":"GCV includes the latent heat of the water vapour formed in combustion; NCV excludes it (NCV = GCV − latent heat of water vapour).","explain":"Hydrogen in the fuel burns to water and any fuel moisture also evaporates. In GCV (higher heating value) that vapour is assumed condensed, so its latent heat is counted. In practice flue gas leaves as vapour, so NCV is the heat actually usable. The difference therefore arises from water vapour/moisture, not from sulphur or ash.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P2 Q45; BEE 2025 P2 Q9, Q48","order":2000},
{"id":"p2-002","paper":2,"topic":"Fuels","kind":"fact","front":"What is the flash point of a fuel oil?","back":"The lowest temperature at which the fuel vapour momentarily ignites (flashes) when a flame is applied.","explain":"Flash point is a safety/storage property, not the temperature of sustained burning (that is the fire point) or of spontaneous ignition (auto-ignition temperature). Furnace oil has a relatively high flash point, which is why it is safe to store and preheat.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P2 Q19","order":2001},
{"id":"p2-003","paper":2,"topic":"Fuels","kind":"fact","front":"What is the pour point of a fuel oil and why does it matter?","back":"The lowest temperature at which the oil will still flow; it tells the minimum temperature at which oil can be stored and pumped.","explain":"Below the pour point the oil gels and cannot be pumped. Heavy oils like furnace oil therefore need tank heating so the oil stays comfortably above its pour point during storage and transfer.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":2002},
{"id":"p2-004","paper":2,"topic":"Fuels","kind":"fact","front":"Why is viscosity of a liquid fuel important for combustion?","back":"It governs atomisation at the burner – oil must be heated to the right viscosity to atomise into fine droplets.","explain":"High viscosity gives large droplets, poor atomisation, incomplete combustion, carbon deposits and smoke. Furnace oil is preheated before the burner to bring its viscosity down to what the burner needs. Viscosity also affects pumping and handling.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P2 Q36","order":2003},
{"id":"p2-005","paper":2,"topic":"Fuels","kind":"fact","front":"Which type of pump is best suited for transferring viscous liquid fuels such as furnace oil?","back":"A positive-displacement gear pump.","explain":"Centrifugal pumps lose efficiency and head sharply as viscosity rises. Gear (positive-displacement) pumps deliver a near-constant flow regardless of viscosity, so they are the standard choice for heavy fuel oil.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P2 Q22","order":2004},
{"id":"p2-006","paper":2,"topic":"Fuels","kind":"fact","front":"What are the main drawbacks of high sulphur content in fuel?","back":"Corrosion (sulphuric acid formation below the acid dew point) and air pollution (SO₂/SO₃).","explain":"Sulphur burns to SO₂ and some SO₃; with water vapour these form sulphuric acid that condenses on cold surfaces such as economisers, air preheaters and chimneys. This sets a minimum safe flue gas exit temperature and limits how much waste heat can be recovered.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P2 Q35","order":2005},
{"id":"p2-007","paper":2,"topic":"Fuels","kind":"fact","front":"What does proximate analysis of coal report, and what does ultimate analysis report?","back":"Proximate: moisture, volatile matter, ash and fixed carbon. Ultimate: elemental C, H, N, S, O (plus ash and moisture).","explain":"Proximate analysis is quick and tells how the coal will behave on a grate or in a furnace. Ultimate analysis gives the elemental composition needed to calculate theoretical air, flue gas quantity and indirect boiler efficiency. Proximate analysis does not give % carbon or % hydrogen.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P2 Q47","order":2006},
{"id":"p2-008","paper":2,"topic":"Fuels","kind":"fact","front":"What is volatile matter in coal and how does it affect combustion?","back":"The gases (methane, hydrocarbons, H₂, CO, tar) driven off when coal is heated; it aids ignition and flame length.","explain":"Higher volatile matter makes coal easier to ignite and gives a longer flame, and it needs adequate secondary air for complete burning. Volatile matter is distinct from fixed carbon, which is the solid carbon remaining after volatiles are driven off.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P2 Q8","order":2007},
{"id":"p2-009","paper":2,"topic":"Combustion","kind":"fact","front":"What are the '3 T's of good combustion?","back":"Time, Temperature and Turbulence.","explain":"Complete combustion needs enough residence time for the reactions, a temperature high enough to ignite and sustain combustion, and turbulence to mix fuel intimately with oxygen. Weakness in any one forces the operator to use more excess air.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":2008},
{"id":"p2-010","paper":2,"topic":"Combustion","kind":"fact","front":"What is the theoretical (stoichiometric) air needed to burn 1 kg of carbon completely?","back":"About 11.6 kg of air.","explain":"C + O₂ → CO₂: 12 kg C needs 32 kg O₂, so 1 kg C needs 2.67 kg O₂. Air contains about 23% O₂ by mass, so air = 2.67/0.23 ≈ 11.6 kg. Example: 30 kg of carbon needs about 348 kg of air.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P2 Q11, L3; BEE 2025 P2 S3","order":2009},
{"id":"p2-011","paper":2,"topic":"Combustion","kind":"fact","front":"How much stoichiometric air is required per kg of hydrogen, and per kg of sulphur?","back":"Hydrogen: about 34.8 kg air/kg; sulphur: about 4.35 kg air/kg.","explain":"2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O: 4 kg H₂ needs 32 kg O₂, so 8 kg O₂ per kg H₂ → 8/0.23 = 34.8 kg air. S + O₂ → SO₂: 32 kg S needs 32 kg O₂, so 1 kg O₂ per kg S → 1/0.23 = 4.35 kg air. Hydrogen therefore needs the most air per kg of fuel.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P2 Q3; BEE 2025 P2 S3","order":2010},
{"id":"p2-012","paper":2,"topic":"Combustion","kind":"fact","front":"Write the formula for theoretical air per kg of fuel from its ultimate analysis.","back":"Theoretical air (kg/kg fuel) = [2.67 C + 8 H + 1 S − O] / 0.23, with C, H, S, O as mass fractions.","explain":"Each element's oxygen demand is summed, the oxygen already in the fuel is subtracted, and the total is divided by the mass fraction of O₂ in air (0.23). A typical furnace oil works out to about 14 kg air per kg fuel.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P2 L2(c)","order":2011},
{"id":"p2-013","paper":2,"topic":"Combustion","kind":"fact","front":"When pure hydrogen burns with stoichiometric air, what is the %CO₂ and %N₂ in the dry flue gas?","back":"0% CO₂ and 100% N₂ (dry basis).","explain":"Hydrogen burns only to water. On a dry basis the water is removed, so with exactly stoichiometric air the only gas left is the nitrogen that came with the air.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P2 Q9; BEE 2025 P2 S3","order":2012},
{"id":"p2-014","paper":2,"topic":"Combustion","kind":"fact","front":"How is % excess air calculated from the O₂ content of the flue gas?","back":"Excess air (%) = O₂% / (21 − O₂%) × 100.","explain":"This is the standard BEE approximation using dry flue gas O₂. Example: 5% O₂ gives 5/16 × 100 = 31.25% excess air; 3% O₂ gives 16.7%; 11% O₂ gives 110%. Actual air = theoretical air × (1 + EA/100).","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P2 Q43; BEE 2025 P2 L6","order":2013},
{"id":"p2-015","paper":2,"topic":"Combustion","kind":"fact","front":"Which type of fuel needs the least excess air: gaseous, liquid or solid?","back":"Gaseous fuels (e.g. natural gas, LPG/propane) need the least excess air; solid fuels need the most.","explain":"Gases mix readily and completely with air at molecular level. Liquids must first be atomised and vaporised, and solids (coal, rice husk) present limited surface area, so they need progressively more excess air to achieve complete combustion.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P2 Q20","order":2014},
{"id":"p2-016","paper":2,"topic":"Combustion","kind":"fact","front":"A flue gas analysis shows high CO₂ (close to theoretical maximum). What does it indicate?","back":"Efficient combustion with low excess air.","explain":"For a given fuel, CO₂ in flue gas is maximum at stoichiometric conditions. As excess air increases the CO₂ is diluted and falls. So a high CO₂ (with negligible CO) means near-optimum air, while a low CO₂ suggests high excess air or air ingress.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P2 Q12","order":2015},
{"id":"p2-017","paper":2,"topic":"Combustion","kind":"fact","front":"Which instrument/sensor is used for continuous measurement of O₂ in flue gas?","back":"Zirconium oxide (zirconia) probe.","explain":"A zirconia cell generates a voltage proportional to the oxygen partial-pressure difference between the flue gas and reference air. Portable checks often use a Fyrite kit or electronic flue gas analysers. Continuous O₂ trim lets the air-fuel ratio be controlled automatically.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P2 Q10","order":2016},
{"id":"p2-018","paper":2,"topic":"Combustion","kind":"problem","front":"A furnace burns 400 litres/h of furnace oil (specific gravity 0.94). Ultimate analysis by mass: C 85%, H 11.5%, S 2.5%, O 1%. Flue gas O₂ (dry) is measured at 4%. Taking air as 23% O₂ by mass, find (a) theoretical air per kg fuel, (b) % excess air, and (c) actual combustion air supplied per hour.","back":"(a) ≈ 13.92 kg air/kg fuel; (b) ≈ 23.5% excess air; (c) ≈ 6,466 kg air/h","explain":"Theoretical air comes from the oxygen demand of C, H and S minus the oxygen already in the fuel, divided by 0.23. Excess air is found from flue gas O₂ using O₂/(21 − O₂). Actual air per hour = fuel mass flow × theoretical air × (1 + EA). Remember to convert litres to kg with specific gravity first – a very common slip.","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the oxygen needed per kg of fuel.","answer":"O₂ = 0.85×32/12 + 0.115×8 + 0.025×1 − 0.01 = 2.267 + 0.920 + 0.025 − 0.01 = 3.202 kg/kg fuel","explain":"C + O₂ → CO₂ needs 32/12 kg O₂ per kg C; H₂ needs 8 kg O₂ per kg; S needs 1 kg O₂ per kg; subtract fuel oxygen."},{"prompt":"Convert oxygen to theoretical air.","answer":"Theoretical air = 3.202 / 0.23 = 13.92 kg air/kg fuel","explain":"Air is about 23% O₂ by mass."},{"prompt":"Calculate % excess air from 4% O₂.","answer":"EA = 4 / (21 − 4) × 100 = 23.5%","explain":"Standard BEE relation EA = O₂/(21 − O₂)."},{"prompt":"Find actual air per kg fuel.","answer":"Actual air = 13.92 × (1 + 0.235) = 17.20 kg/kg fuel","explain":"Actual air = theoretical air × (1 + EA/100)."},{"prompt":"Find fuel mass flow and hourly air.","answer":"Fuel = 400 × 0.94 = 376 kg/h; Air = 376 × 17.20 ≈ 6,466 kg/h","explain":"Litres × specific gravity = kg."}],"flash":false,"source":"Based on BEE 2024 P2 L2(c)","order":2017},
{"id":"p2-019","paper":2,"topic":"Boilers","kind":"fact","front":"Under the Indian Boilers Act/Regulations, what minimum capacity of closed vessel generating steam under pressure is treated as a 'boiler'?","back":"A closed vessel exceeding 22.75 litres capacity.","explain":"Any closed vessel larger than 22.75 litres used to generate steam under pressure, including its mountings, falls under IBR and requires registration and inspection.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P2 Q6","order":2018},
{"id":"p2-020","paper":2,"topic":"Boilers","kind":"fact","front":"Define the evaporation ratio of a boiler.","back":"Kilograms of steam generated per kilogram of fuel consumed.","explain":"Also called steam-to-fuel ratio, it is a quick daily performance indicator. Example: 24 t steam with 2 t fuel gives 12. Typical values are roughly 6 for coal-fired and 13 for oil-fired boilers; a falling ratio warns of fouling, excess air or other losses. It must be compared at similar feed water temperature and steam pressure.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P2 Q33, L3","order":2019},
{"id":"p2-021","paper":2,"topic":"Boilers","kind":"fact","front":"What is the formula for boiler efficiency by the direct method?","back":"η = Steam flow × (h_steam − h_feedwater) / (Fuel flow × GCV) × 100.","explain":"Also called the input-output method. It needs only steam flow, steam pressure/temperature, feed water temperature, fuel flow and GCV. Its weakness is that it gives no breakdown of where the losses occur and is sensitive to errors in measuring steam and fuel flow.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P2 S4, L4","order":2020},
{"id":"p2-022","paper":2,"topic":"Boilers","kind":"fact","front":"List the losses evaluated in the indirect (heat-loss) method of boiler efficiency.","back":"Dry flue gas loss; loss due to hydrogen in fuel; moisture in fuel; moisture in combustion air; partial combustion (CO); surface radiation & convection; unburnt carbon in fly ash and bottom ash.","explain":"Efficiency = 100 − sum of losses (%). Fuel ultimate analysis, flue gas O₂/CO₂ and CO, flue gas and ambient temperatures, and ash unburnt carbon are needed. The method shows which loss dominates, so it guides corrective action.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":2021},
{"id":"p2-023","paper":2,"topic":"Boilers","kind":"fact","front":"Which is usually the single largest heat loss in an oil- or coal-fired boiler?","back":"Dry flue gas (stack) loss.","explain":"Stack loss depends on the mass of flue gas (set by excess air) and the temperature difference between flue gas and ambient. Reducing excess air and recovering heat with economisers/air preheaters are therefore the main efficiency levers.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P2 Q7","order":2022},
{"id":"p2-024","paper":2,"topic":"Boilers","kind":"fact","front":"What is the function of an economiser in a boiler?","back":"It recovers heat from exit flue gas to preheat the boiler feed water.","explain":"Hotter feed water means less fuel is needed to raise it to saturation. A useful rule of thumb: every 6 °C rise in feed water temperature saves about 1% fuel; equivalently, about 22 °C reduction in flue gas temperature raises boiler efficiency by about 1%.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P2 Q14, Q15; BEE 2025 P2 L3","order":2023},
{"id":"p2-025","paper":2,"topic":"Boilers","kind":"fact","front":"What is the function of an air preheater (APH) in a boiler?","back":"It recovers heat from flue gas to preheat combustion air.","explain":"Hot combustion air improves combustion and reduces the fuel needed. A commonly quoted thumb rule is roughly 1% fuel saving for every 20 °C rise in combustion air temperature. APHs are standard in pulverised fuel and FBC boilers.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":2024},
{"id":"p2-026","paper":2,"topic":"Boilers","kind":"fact","front":"What sets the lower limit to which flue gas can be cooled in an economiser or air preheater?","back":"The acid dew point of the flue gas (governed by sulphur content).","explain":"If metal surfaces fall below the acid dew point, sulphuric acid condenses and corrodes them. High-sulphur fuels such as furnace oil therefore need higher exit gas temperatures than natural gas, which can be cooled much further.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":2025},
{"id":"p2-027","paper":2,"topic":"Boilers","kind":"fact","front":"What are the critical pressure and temperature of water/steam?","back":"About 221.2 bar and 374.15 °C.","explain":"At the critical point the densities of liquid and vapour become equal and latent heat becomes zero; above it there is no distinct boiling. Supercritical boilers operate above this pressure, so they are once-through units with no steam drum.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P2 Q34; BEE 2025 P2 Q13, Q25","order":2026}
]$cards$::jsonb);
